import QtslAux.QuantumStates
import Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.RealDeriv
import Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.ExpDeriv

/-! # Cauchy-determinacy under a mechanism profile

The time-slice axiom (Haag–Schroer 1962, J. Math. Phys. 3, 248; in its
locally covariant form Brunetti–Fredenhagen–Verch,
arXiv:math-ph/0112041, Definition 2.1(iii)) says the observables near a
Cauchy surface already determine the observables of the whole region —
it is the one axiom of the locally covariant package that is about
*dynamics*: Fewster and Verch derive relative Cauchy evolution from it
and read the stress-energy tensor off its derivative
(arXiv:1504.00586, §3.4).  This module states and proves the honest
finite-dimensional form of that axiom over the derived diamond site,
scoped exactly to what a mechanism can purchase at matrix grade, in
five movements.

* **The Heisenberg net of a mechanism.**  Matter that persists in time
  is a family of worldlines `w : ι → T → X` carrying observable factors
  `B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)`; a mechanism is a family of
  algebra automorphisms `τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)`
  (the one-parameter groups generated by the Hamiltonian part of a GKSL
  profile are the intended instances, and the concrete dress below is
  one).  `heisenbergNet w B τ` assigns to a diamond the subalgebra
  generated by the mechanism-evolved factors of the worldline crossings
  it contains — the net generated by time-evolved local observables, in
  the Heisenberg picture.

* **Cauchy-determinacy.**  `CommonSlice w t₀ E` says the diamond `E`
  covers the common-time slice of the worldline family at `t₀`.  When
  the factors jointly exhaust the matter algebra (`⨆ i, B i = ⊤` — the
  finite shadow of "the Cauchy surface carries complete initial data"),
  a slice-covering subdiamond already carries the whole algebra
  (`heisenbergNet_alg_eq_top_of_commonSlice`), so inclusion upgrades to
  equality (`heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice`) — Definition
  2.1(iii)'s conclusion shape — and the state-presheaf restriction of
  `QtslAux.QuantumStates` along the slice inclusion is a *bijection*
  (`heisenbergNet_commonSlice_restriction_bijective`): evolution under
  the mechanism determines the state assignment on the diamond from its
  data on the Cauchy-slice-shaped subdiamond, on the whole fibers —
  no state is preferred, per the set-valued doctrine (BFV §5.1).

* **The contrast pair: what the mechanism buys.**  The landed kinematic
  `qubitNet` has point-supported matter (sites at time 0 only): at the
  slice diamond `Eslice` — which covers the worldline slice at time 1
  and contains neither native site — its local algebra is trivial
  (`qubitNet_alg_Eslice`) and its restriction map is *not injective*:
  the Bell and maximally-mixed classes collapse
  (`qubitNet_Eslice_restriction_not_injective`).  Every Heisenberg net
  over the same worldlines satisfies determinacy at the same inclusion,
  for every mechanism (`heisenbergQubitNet_alg_Eslice`).  Determinacy
  is purchased by matter-persistence plus mechanism-invertibility, not
  by site bookkeeping; and the site-exhaustion surrogate of the
  time-slice axiom is recovered from this theorem by *freezing* the
  net — constant worldlines, identity mechanism — which computes to the
  kinematic net exactly (`heisenbergNet_const_id_alg_eq_qubitNet`,
  `qubitNet_alg_eq_of_sites_mem`): the surrogate is the frozen shadow
  of Cauchy-determinacy at its complete-data grade.

* **The mechanism profile.**  The concrete dress is the σz ⊗ σz
  coupling (`isingH`, the canonical entangling two-qubit Hamiltonian,
  written directly in its eigenbasis), whose unitary flow `isingU` is a
  one-parameter group (`isingU_add`) of automorphisms (`isingFlow`)
  solving the master equation of the Lindblad-free GKSL profile of
  `QtslAux.OpenQuantum` (`hasDerivAt_isingEvolution` — the `hflow`
  hypothesis shape of `fence_transport`, discharged for a concrete
  interacting mechanism; GKSL: Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan,
  J. Math. Phys. 17, 821 (1976), and Lindblad, Commun. Math. Phys. 48,
  119 (1976)).  The flow preserves statehood
  (`isingEvolution_trace`, `isingEvolution_posSemidef`), and the
  headline instances (`isingQubitNet_slice_determinacy` and kin) tie
  determinacy to this profile; covariance transport from
  `QtslAux.QuantumCovariance` then re-derives determinacy at every
  Minkowski translate by pure transport
  (`isingQubitNet_translate_slice_determinacy`) — no slice or matrix
  fact re-proven.

* **The reversibility fence.**  The algebra-transport formulation is
  deliberately scoped to the *reversible* (Lindblad-free) profiles, and
  the boundary is a theorem pair: the Lindblad-free Heisenberg
  generator is a derivation (`gkslDag_mul_of_isEmpty`), while a single
  Lindblad operator breaks the Leibniz law
  (`exists_gkslDag_not_leibniz`) — dissipative Heisenberg maps are not
  algebra morphisms even infinitesimally, so no Heisenberg net rides
  them.  Forward state determinacy for dissipative profiles is
  ODE-uniqueness territory, out of scope and named.

**Scope, stated deliberately (the kinematic-fragment register).**
Everything here is the *matter-worldline shadow* of the time-slice
axiom, at matrix grade: a Cauchy surface meets every inextendible
causal curve, while `CommonSlice` meets every matter worldline at a
single common time.  Flatness is load-bearing: tilted slices (a
different time per worldline) genuinely fail for
information-transporting mechanisms — a dynamics that swaps the
subsystems between the two slice times makes both slice readings the
same factor — and are a named residue, not formalized.  On supports
with cross-worldline causal links, maximal chains of the induced order
zigzag across worldlines and do not respect the factor structure this
proof transports, so the chain-shaped slice vocabulary (the
`chain_linearization_unique` world of `QtslAux.QuantumRecords`) is
deliberately not bridged here.  Exhaustive factors are the honest
hypothesis, not a convenience: without complete initial data on the
slice, a mechanism spreads a single factor across times and inclusion
does not upgrade to equality.  Nothing here claims "the same physics
in all spacetimes": Fewster and Verch (arXiv:1106.4785) prove that
property fails for the full class of locally covariant theories, and
their repair (dynamical locality) lives on the far side of relative
Cauchy evolution — which needs genuine dynamics on morphism images and
is where this fragment's boundary stands. -- [classical]
-/

namespace Qtsl
namespace QuantumDeterminacy

open CategoryTheory Matrix DiamondSite QuantumDescent QuantumStates
open QuantumCovariance OpenQuantum
open scoped ComplexOrder

variable {X : Type*} [PartialOrder X]
variable {n : Type*} [Fintype n] [DecidableEq n]
variable {T ι : Type*}

/-! ## The Heisenberg net of a mechanism

No structure is demanded of the time index `T` or the system index `ι`:
the determinacy core consumes none of it.  The mechanism profiles
supply `T = ℝ` with its group structure; the group law is genuinely not
consumed at this grade (it would enter for tilted slices, which fail —
see the module docstring). -/

/-- The Heisenberg net of a mechanism: a diamond reads the subalgebra
generated by the mechanism-evolved factors of the worldline crossings
it contains — the net generated by time-evolved local observables.
Isotony is monotonicity of the crossing set. -/
noncomputable def heisenbergNet (w : ι → T → X)
    (B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ))
    (τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)) : ObservableNet X n where
  alg D := ⨆ p : {q : ι × T // inDiamond (w q.1 q.2) D},
    (B p.1.1).map (τ p.1.2).toAlgHom
  isotone {_E D} h := iSup_le fun p =>
    le_iSup (fun q : {q : ι × T // inDiamond (w q.1 q.2) D} =>
      (B q.1.1).map (τ q.1.2).toAlgHom) ⟨p.1, inDiamond_mono h p.2⟩

/-- A worldline crossing contributes its evolved factor to the local
algebra. -/
theorem factor_le_heisenbergNet_alg {w : ι → T → X}
    {B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)}
    {τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)} {D : Diamond X}
    {i : ι} {t : T} (h : inDiamond (w i t) D) :
    (B i).map (τ t).toAlgHom ≤ (heisenbergNet w B τ).alg D :=
  le_iSup (fun q : {q : ι × T // inDiamond (w q.1 q.2) D} =>
    (B q.1.1).map (τ q.1.2).toAlgHom) ⟨(i, t), h⟩

/-- The diamond `E` covers the common-time slice of the worldline
family at `t₀`.  This is the matter-worldline shadow of "contains a
Cauchy surface": a Cauchy surface meets every inextendible causal
curve; a common slice meets every matter worldline, at one shared
time.  Flatness (the shared time) is load-bearing, and the chain-shaped
slice vocabulary is deliberately not bridged here — see the module
docstring for both fences. -/
def CommonSlice (w : ι → T → X) (t₀ : T) (E : Diamond X) : Prop :=
  ∀ i, inDiamond (w i t₀) E

/-- A common slice of a subdiamond is a common slice of any
superdiamond. -/
theorem CommonSlice.mono {w : ι → T → X} {t₀ : T} {E D : Diamond X}
    (hsl : CommonSlice w t₀ E) (h : E ≤ D) : CommonSlice w t₀ D :=
  fun i => inDiamond_mono h (hsl i)

/-! ## Cauchy-determinacy, algebra grade -/

/-- **A slice-covering diamond carries the whole algebra.**  When the
factors jointly exhaust the matter algebra — the finite shadow of "the
Cauchy surface carries complete initial data" — the mechanism-evolved
factors at the common slice time already generate everything: the
mechanism at the slice time is an automorphism, so it moves the
exhausting family onto an exhausting family.  Kinematic-fragment
register: this is where invertibility of the mechanism is consumed
(dissipative profiles are fenced off by `exists_gkslDag_not_leibniz`
below). -/
theorem heisenbergNet_alg_eq_top_of_commonSlice {w : ι → T → X}
    {B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)}
    {τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)}
    (hexh : ⨆ i, B i = ⊤) {E : Diamond X} {t₀ : T}
    (hsl : CommonSlice w t₀ E) :
    (heisenbergNet w B τ).alg E = ⊤ := by
  have htop : (⊤ : Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)).map (τ t₀).toAlgHom = ⊤ := by
    rw [eq_top_iff]
    intro a _
    exact Subalgebra.mem_map.mpr
      ⟨(τ t₀).symm a, Algebra.mem_top, (τ t₀).apply_symm_apply a⟩
  rw [eq_top_iff, ← htop, ← hexh,
    (Subalgebra.gc_map_comap (τ t₀).toAlgHom).l_iSup]
  exact iSup_le fun i => factor_le_heisenbergNet_alg (hsl i)

/-- **Cauchy-determinacy under a mechanism, algebra grade** — the
time-slice conclusion shape (Brunetti–Fredenhagen–Verch Def. 2.1(iii)):
a subdiamond covering a common slice of exhaustive matter carries the
same algebra as any superdiamond.  Isotony upgraded to equality by the
mechanism.  Kinematic-fragment register: this is determinacy for the
toy's finite matter content under a reversible mechanism, never a
same-physics claim (Fewster–Verch arXiv:1106.4785). -/
theorem heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice {w : ι → T → X}
    {B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)}
    {τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)}
    (hexh : ⨆ i, B i = ⊤) {E D : Diamond X} (hle : E ≤ D) {t₀ : T}
    (hsl : CommonSlice w t₀ E) :
    (heisenbergNet w B τ).alg E = (heisenbergNet w B τ).alg D := by
  rw [heisenbergNet_alg_eq_top_of_commonSlice hexh hsl,
    heisenbergNet_alg_eq_top_of_commonSlice hexh (hsl.mono hle)]

/-! ## Cauchy-determinacy at the state presheaf

Determinacy at the state object: equal local algebras make the
state-presheaf restriction a bijection on the whole fibers.  The
mechanism rides the *net* (Heisenberg picture); no constraint on the
state classes is needed, and none is imposed — the set-valued doctrine
of BFV §5.1 (no preferred state) holds by construction, exactly as in
`QtslAux.QuantumStates`. -/

/-- On a diamond with trivial algebra, all states are locally equal:
every element is a scalar, and trace-one states agree on scalars. -/
theorem locallyEqual_of_alg_eq_bot {N : ObservableNet X n} {E : Diamond X}
    (hbot : N.alg E = ⊥) {ρ σ : Matrix n n ℂ}
    (hρ : ρ.trace = 1) (hσ : σ.trace = 1) :
    LocallyEqual N E ρ σ := by
  intro a ha
  rw [hbot, Algebra.mem_bot] at ha
  obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := ha
  rw [Algebra.algebraMap_eq_smul_one, Matrix.mul_smul, Matrix.mul_smul,
    mul_one, mul_one, Matrix.trace_smul, Matrix.trace_smul, hρ, hσ]

/-- Equal local algebras identify the local-equality relations. -/
theorem locallyEqual_congr_of_alg_eq {N : ObservableNet X n}
    {E D : Diamond X} (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D) {ρ σ : Matrix n n ℂ} :
    LocallyEqual N D ρ σ ↔ LocallyEqual N E ρ σ := by
  unfold LocallyEqual
  rw [halg]

/-- Equal local algebras make the state-presheaf fibers equivalent.
(The inclusion hypothesis is carried so that the equivalence is
literally the restriction map — `restrictionEquivOfAlgEq_apply`.) -/
def restrictionEquivOfAlgEq {N : ObservableNet X n} {E D : Diamond X}
    (_hle : E ≤ D) (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D) :
    (StatePresheaf N).obj (Opposite.op D) ≃
      (StatePresheaf N).obj (Opposite.op E) :=
  Quotient.congrRight (r := stateSetoid N D) (r' := stateSetoid N E)
    fun ρ σ => locallyEqual_congr_of_alg_eq halg (ρ := ρ.1) (σ := σ.1)

@[simp] theorem restrictionEquivOfAlgEq_apply {N : ObservableNet X n}
    {E D : Diamond X} (hle : E ≤ D) (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D)
    (x : (StatePresheaf N).obj (Opposite.op D)) :
    restrictionEquivOfAlgEq hle halg x =
      (StatePresheaf N).map (homOfLE hle).op x := by
  refine stateClass_ind (motive := fun y =>
    restrictionEquivOfAlgEq hle halg y =
      (StatePresheaf N).map (homOfLE hle).op y) (fun ρ => ?_) x
  rfl

/-- **Equal local algebras make the restriction a bijection** — full
determinacy at the state object: data on the subdiamond extends
uniquely to the superdiamond. -/
theorem statePresheaf_restriction_bijective_of_alg_eq
    {N : ObservableNet X n} {E D : Diamond X} (hle : E ≤ D)
    (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D) :
    Function.Bijective
      (fun x => (StatePresheaf N).map (homOfLE hle).op x) := by
  have h : (fun x => (StatePresheaf N).map (homOfLE hle).op x) =
      ⇑(restrictionEquivOfAlgEq hle halg) :=
    funext fun x => (restrictionEquivOfAlgEq_apply hle halg x).symm
  rw [h]
  exact (restrictionEquivOfAlgEq hle halg).bijective

/-- **Cauchy-determinacy under a mechanism, state grade**: evolution
under the mechanism determines the state assignment on a diamond from
its data on a common-slice-covering subdiamond — the state-presheaf
restriction along the Cauchy inclusion is invertible, on the whole
fibers.  Kinematic-fragment register as in
`heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice`. -/
theorem heisenbergNet_commonSlice_restriction_bijective {w : ι → T → X}
    {B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)}
    {τ : T → (Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ)}
    (hexh : ⨆ i, B i = ⊤) {E D : Diamond X} (hle : E ≤ D) {t₀ : T}
    (hsl : CommonSlice w t₀ E) :
    Function.Bijective (fun x =>
      (StatePresheaf (heisenbergNet w B τ)).map (homOfLE hle).op x) :=
  statePresheaf_restriction_bijective_of_alg_eq hle
    (heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice hexh hle hsl)

/-! ## Transport: determinacy moves along order isomorphisms

Consumed from `QtslAux.QuantumCovariance`, never re-proven.  No
chronology hypothesis appears: the transported statement is algebra-
and states-only, and the covariance module's chronology hypotheses
enter only where topology or interiority transport — which determinacy
does not consume. -/

/-- Algebra-equality statements transport along an order isomorphism of
event carriers, by pure pushforward bookkeeping. -/
theorem alg_eq_transport {Y : Type*} [PartialOrder Y] (f : X ≃o Y)
    {N : ObservableNet X n} {E D : Diamond X}
    (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D) :
    (N.map f).alg (E.map f) = (N.map f).alg (D.map f) := by
  rw [ObservableNet.map_alg_map, ObservableNet.map_alg_map, halg]

/-- Restriction-bijectivity transports along an order isomorphism. -/
theorem restriction_bijective_transport {Y : Type*} [PartialOrder Y]
    (f : X ≃o Y) {N : ObservableNet X n} {E D : Diamond X} (hle : E ≤ D)
    (halg : N.alg E = N.alg D) :
    Function.Bijective (fun x => (StatePresheaf (N.map f)).map
      (homOfLE ((Diamond.map_le_map_iff f).mpr hle)).op x) :=
  statePresheaf_restriction_bijective_of_alg_eq
    ((Diamond.map_le_map_iff f).mpr hle) (alg_eq_transport f halg)

/-! ## The two-qubit Minkowski dress, and the contrast pair -/

private theorem iSup_fin_two {α : Type*} [CompleteLattice α]
    (f : Fin 2 → α) : (⨆ i, f i) = f 0 ⊔ f 1 := by
  refine le_antisymm (iSup_le fun i => ?_)
    (sup_le (le_iSup f 0) (le_iSup f 1))
  fin_cases i
  · exact le_sup_left
  · exact le_sup_right

/-- The two qubit worldlines: static spatial positions (those of the
landed sites `p`, `q`), persistent in time. -/
def qubitWorldline : Fin 2 → ℝ → Mink ℝ :=
  fun i t => ⟨t, ![(-1 : ℝ), 1] i⟩

theorem qubitWorldline_zero_zero : qubitWorldline 0 0 = p := rfl

theorem qubitWorldline_one_zero : qubitWorldline 1 0 = q := rfl

/-- The worldline factors: each qubit carries its full tensor factor —
complete initial data per subsystem. -/
def qubitFactors : Fin 2 → Subalgebra ℂ TwoQubits :=
  ![leftFactor, rightFactor]

/-- The two qubit factors jointly exhaust the two-qubit algebra: the
linear failure of the pair-cover (`span_union_ne_top`) is a failure of
*linear* generation only — multiplicatively the factors generate
everything (`adjoin_union_eq_top`), which is what the lattice join
reads. -/
theorem leftFactor_sup_rightFactor : leftFactor ⊔ rightFactor = ⊤ := by
  refine le_antisymm le_top ?_
  rw [← adjoin_union_eq_top]
  exact Algebra.adjoin_le (Set.union_subset
    (SetLike.coe_subset_coe.mpr le_sup_left)
    (SetLike.coe_subset_coe.mpr le_sup_right))

theorem qubitFactors_exhaustive : ⨆ i, qubitFactors i = ⊤ := by
  rw [iSup_fin_two]
  exact leftFactor_sup_rightFactor

/-- The Heisenberg net of the two qubit worldlines under an arbitrary
mechanism. -/
noncomputable abbrev heisenbergQubitNet
    (τ : ℝ → (TwoQubits ≃ₐ[ℂ] TwoQubits)) :
    ObservableNet (Mink ℝ) (Fin 2 × Fin 2) :=
  heisenbergNet qubitWorldline qubitFactors τ

/-- The slice diamond: covers the common-time slice of the qubit
worldlines at time 1, and contains neither native site. -/
def Eslice : Diamond (Mink ℝ) :=
  ⟨⟨0, 0⟩, ⟨2, 0⟩, by norm_num [Mink.le_def, Real.norm_eq_abs]⟩

theorem Eslice_le_Dstar : Eslice ≤ Dstar := by
  constructor
  · show ‖(0 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (0 : ℝ) - (-3)
    rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num
  · show ‖(0 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (3 : ℝ) - 2
    rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num

/-- `Eslice` covers the qubit worldlines' common slice at time 1 (the
worldline points `⟨1, ±1⟩` sit on its null boundary — `inDiamond` is
causal, not chronological, membership). -/
theorem qubitWorldline_commonSlice_Eslice :
    CommonSlice qubitWorldline 1 Eslice := by
  intro i
  fin_cases i
  · constructor
    · show ‖(-1 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (1 : ℝ) - 0
      rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num
    · show ‖(0 : ℝ) - (-1)‖ ≤ (2 : ℝ) - 1
      rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num
  · constructor
    · show ‖(1 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (1 : ℝ) - 0
      rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num
    · show ‖(0 : ℝ) - 1‖ ≤ (2 : ℝ) - 1
      rw [Real.norm_eq_abs]; norm_num

theorem p_not_mem_Eslice : ¬ inDiamond p Eslice := by
  rintro ⟨h, -⟩
  have : ‖(-1 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (0 : ℝ) - 0 := h
  rw [Real.norm_eq_abs] at this
  norm_num at this

theorem q_not_mem_Eslice : ¬ inDiamond q Eslice := by
  rintro ⟨h, -⟩
  have : ‖(1 : ℝ) - 0‖ ≤ (0 : ℝ) - 0 := h
  rw [Real.norm_eq_abs] at this
  norm_num at this

/-- **The payoff, flow side, for every mechanism**: the slice diamond
at time 1 carries the same algebra as the ambient diamond — genuine
Cauchy-determinacy at a slice time where no native site exists.
Kinematic-fragment register as in the abstract theorem. -/
theorem heisenbergQubitNet_alg_Eslice
    (τ : ℝ → (TwoQubits ≃ₐ[ℂ] TwoQubits)) :
    (heisenbergQubitNet τ).alg Eslice = (heisenbergQubitNet τ).alg Dstar :=
  heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice qubitFactors_exhaustive
    Eslice_le_Dstar qubitWorldline_commonSlice_Eslice

/-- The payoff at the state object: the restriction along
`Eslice ≤ Dstar` is a bijection for every mechanism. -/
theorem heisenbergQubitNet_Eslice_restriction_bijective
    (τ : ℝ → (TwoQubits ≃ₐ[ℂ] TwoQubits)) :
    Function.Bijective (fun x =>
      (StatePresheaf (heisenbergQubitNet τ)).map
        (homOfLE Eslice_le_Dstar).op x) :=
  heisenbergNet_commonSlice_restriction_bijective
    qubitFactors_exhaustive Eslice_le_Dstar
    qubitWorldline_commonSlice_Eslice

open scoped Classical in
/-- The kinematic net is blind at the slice diamond: neither
point-supported site is there. -/
theorem qubitNet_alg_Eslice : qubitNet.alg Eslice = ⊥ := by
  show (if inDiamond p Eslice then _ else _) = ⊥
  rw [if_neg p_not_mem_Eslice, if_neg q_not_mem_Eslice]

set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000 in
/-- **The contrast refusal**: the kinematic `qubitNet` fails
Cauchy-determinacy at the very inclusion where every Heisenberg net
satisfies it — its restriction to the slice diamond is not injective
(the Bell and maximally-mixed classes at the ambient diamond are
distinct, yet collapse at the trivial slice algebra).  Determinacy is
purchased by matter-persistence plus mechanism-invertibility, not by
site bookkeeping: the genuine tier of the time-slice story is not the
site-exhaustion surrogate re-dressed. -/
theorem qubitNet_Eslice_restriction_not_injective :
    ¬ Function.Injective (fun x =>
      (StatePresheaf qubitNet).map (homOfLE Eslice_le_Dstar).op x) := by
  intro hinj
  have himg : (StatePresheaf qubitNet).map (homOfLE Eslice_le_Dstar).op
        (stateClassMk qubitNet Dstar ⟨bell, bell_isState⟩) =
      (StatePresheaf qubitNet).map (homOfLE Eslice_le_Dstar).op
        (stateClassMk qubitNet Dstar ⟨mixed, mixed_isState⟩) := by
    rw [statePresheaf_map_mk, statePresheaf_map_mk]
    exact stateClass_sound
      (locallyEqual_of_alg_eq_bot qubitNet_alg_Eslice bell_trace mixed_trace)
  exact bell_ne_mixed
    ((locallyEqual_iff_eq_of_alg_eq_top qubitNet_Dstar).mp
      (stateClass_exact (hinj himg)))

/-! ## The frozen shadow: the site-exhaustion surrogate re-derived

Freezing the Heisenberg net — constant worldlines, identity
mechanism — computes to the kinematic point-supported net, and
Cauchy-determinacy specializes to the site-exhaustion surrogate of the
time-slice axiom at its complete-data grade.  (The surrogate's
non-exhaustive branches — equal proper algebras at one-site
inclusions — are site bookkeeping outside determinacy's complete-data
scope.) -/

private theorem map_toAlgHom_refl {S : Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)} :
    S.map (AlgEquiv.refl (R := ℂ) (A₁ := Matrix n n ℂ)).toAlgHom = S := by
  ext x
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, hy, rfl⟩
    exact hy
  · intro hx
    exact ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩

set_option maxHeartbeats 800000 in
/-- The frozen Heisenberg net of constant worldlines under the identity
mechanism computes to conditional factor joins — pure site
bookkeeping. -/
theorem heisenbergNet_const_alg [Nonempty T] (x : ι → X)
    (B : ι → Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ)) (D : Diamond X) :
    (heisenbergNet (fun i (_ : T) => x i) B
      (fun _ => AlgEquiv.refl)).alg D =
      ⨆ i, ⨆ _ : inDiamond (x i) D, B i := by
  refine le_antisymm (iSup_le fun pc => ?_) (iSup₂_le fun i h => ?_)
  · exact le_trans (le_of_eq map_toAlgHom_refl)
      (le_iSup₂ (f := fun i (_ : inDiamond (x i) D) => B i) pc.1.1 pc.2)
  · exact le_trans (le_of_eq map_toAlgHom_refl.symm)
      (factor_le_heisenbergNet_alg (w := fun i (_ : T) => x i) (B := B)
        (τ := fun _ => AlgEquiv.refl) (D := D) (i := i)
        (t := Classical.arbitrary T) h)

open scoped Classical in
/-- **The frozen net is the kinematic net**: constant worldlines at the
two sites, identity mechanism, and the Heisenberg net's algebra equals
`qubitNet`'s at every diamond. -/
theorem heisenbergNet_const_id_alg_eq_qubitNet (D : Diamond (Mink ℝ)) :
    (heisenbergNet (fun i (_ : ℝ) => ![p, q] i) qubitFactors
      (fun _ => AlgEquiv.refl)).alg D = qubitNet.alg D := by
  rw [heisenbergNet_const_alg, iSup_fin_two]
  simp only [qubitFactors, Matrix.cons_val_zero, Matrix.cons_val_one]
  show _ = (if inDiamond p D then (if inDiamond q D then ⊤ else leftFactor)
    else (if inDiamond q D then rightFactor else ⊥))
  by_cases hp : inDiamond p D <;> by_cases hq : inDiamond q D
  · rw [if_pos hp, if_pos hq, iSup_pos hp, iSup_pos hq]
    exact leftFactor_sup_rightFactor
  · rw [if_pos hp, if_neg hq, iSup_pos hp, iSup_neg hq, sup_bot_eq]
  · rw [if_neg hp, if_pos hq, iSup_neg hp, iSup_pos hq, bot_sup_eq]
  · rw [if_neg hp, if_neg hq, iSup_neg hp, iSup_neg hq, sup_bot_eq]

/-- **The site-exhaustion surrogate, re-derived as the frozen shadow**:
for the kinematic net, a subdiamond containing both sites carries its
superdiamond's algebra — obtained from Cauchy-determinacy by freezing
the mechanism, not by branch bookkeeping.  (Complete-data grade of the
time-slice surrogate; the axiom's genuine tier is the mechanism form
above.) -/
theorem qubitNet_alg_eq_of_sites_mem {E D : Diamond (Mink ℝ)}
    (hle : E ≤ D) (hp : inDiamond p E) (hq : inDiamond q E) :
    qubitNet.alg E = qubitNet.alg D := by
  rw [← heisenbergNet_const_id_alg_eq_qubitNet E,
    ← heisenbergNet_const_id_alg_eq_qubitNet D]
  refine heisenbergNet_alg_eq_of_commonSlice qubitFactors_exhaustive hle
    (t₀ := 0) ?_
  intro i
  fin_cases i
  · exact hp
  · exact hq

/-! ## The mechanism profile: the σz ⊗ σz coupling and its flow

The concrete reversible mechanism: the canonical entangling two-qubit
Hamiltonian, written directly in its eigenbasis, whose unitary flow is
a one-parameter group of algebra automorphisms solving the
Lindblad-free master equation of `QtslAux.OpenQuantum`. -/

/-- Conjugation by a unitary, as an algebra automorphism. -/
def unitaryConjAlgEquiv (u : Matrix n n ℂ) (hu : u * uᴴ = 1)
    (hu' : uᴴ * u = 1) : Matrix n n ℂ ≃ₐ[ℂ] Matrix n n ℂ where
  toFun x := u * x * uᴴ
  invFun x := uᴴ * x * u
  left_inv x := by
    calc uᴴ * (u * x * uᴴ) * u = (uᴴ * u) * x * (uᴴ * u) := by
          noncomm_ring
      _ = x := by rw [hu', one_mul, mul_one]
  right_inv x := by
    calc u * (uᴴ * x * u) * uᴴ = (u * uᴴ) * x * (u * uᴴ) := by
          noncomm_ring
      _ = x := by rw [hu, one_mul, mul_one]
  map_mul' x y := by
    refine Eq.symm ?_
    calc (u * x * uᴴ) * (u * y * uᴴ) = u * x * (uᴴ * u) * y * uᴴ := by
          noncomm_ring
      _ = u * x * 1 * y * uᴴ := by rw [hu']
      _ = u * (x * y) * uᴴ := by noncomm_ring
  map_add' x y := by noncomm_ring
  commutes' c := by
    show u * algebraMap ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ) c * uᴴ =
      algebraMap ℂ (Matrix n n ℂ) c
    rw [Algebra.algebraMap_eq_smul_one, Matrix.mul_smul, mul_one,
      Matrix.smul_mul, hu]

@[simp] theorem unitaryConjAlgEquiv_apply (u : Matrix n n ℂ)
    (hu : u * uᴴ = 1) (hu' : uᴴ * u = 1) (x : Matrix n n ℂ) :
    unitaryConjAlgEquiv u hu hu' x = u * x * uᴴ :=
  rfl

/-- The σz ⊗ σz eigenphases: `+1` on the aligned computational basis
states, `−1` on the anti-aligned ones. -/
def isingPhase : Fin 2 × Fin 2 → ℝ := fun ik => if ik.1 = ik.2 then 1 else -1

/-- The σz ⊗ σz coupling Hamiltonian, written directly in its
eigenbasis: the canonical entangling two-qubit interaction. -/
noncomputable def isingH : TwoQubits :=
  Matrix.diagonal (fun ik => (isingPhase ik : ℂ))

theorem isingH_isHermitian : isingH.IsHermitian := by
  show isingHᴴ = isingH
  unfold isingH
  rw [Matrix.diagonal_conjTranspose]
  congr 1
  funext ik
  simp [Pi.star_apply, Complex.conj_ofReal]

/-- The unitary flow of the coupling: diagonal phases
`exp (−i t · isingPhase)`. -/
noncomputable def isingU (t : ℝ) : TwoQubits :=
  Matrix.diagonal (fun ik => Complex.exp (-Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik))

private theorem star_exp_isingArg (t : ℝ) (ik : Fin 2 × Fin 2) :
    star (Complex.exp (-Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik)) =
      Complex.exp (Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik) := by
  rw [Complex.star_def, ← Complex.exp_conj]
  congr 1
  simp [map_mul, map_neg, Complex.conj_I, Complex.conj_ofReal]

theorem isingU_zero : isingU 0 = 1 := by
  unfold isingU
  rw [← Matrix.diagonal_one]
  congr 1
  funext ik
  norm_num

theorem isingU_mul_conjTranspose_self (t : ℝ) :
    isingU t * (isingU t)ᴴ = 1 := by
  unfold isingU
  rw [Matrix.diagonal_conjTranspose, Matrix.diagonal_mul_diagonal,
    ← Matrix.diagonal_one]
  congr 1
  funext ik
  rw [Pi.star_apply, star_exp_isingArg, ← Complex.exp_add,
    show -Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik + Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik = 0
      from by ring,
    Complex.exp_zero]

theorem isingU_conjTranspose_mul_self (t : ℝ) :
    (isingU t)ᴴ * isingU t = 1 := by
  unfold isingU
  rw [Matrix.diagonal_conjTranspose, Matrix.diagonal_mul_diagonal,
    ← Matrix.diagonal_one]
  congr 1
  funext ik
  rw [Pi.star_apply, star_exp_isingArg, ← Complex.exp_add,
    show Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik + -Complex.I * t * isingPhase ik = 0
      from by ring,
    Complex.exp_zero]

/-- The flow is a one-parameter group.  (The group law is
mechanism-profile honesty: the determinacy theorems above deliberately
do not consume it.) -/
theorem isingU_add (s t : ℝ) : isingU (s + t) = isingU s * isingU t := by
  unfold isingU
  rw [Matrix.diagonal_mul_diagonal]
  congr 1
  funext ik
  rw [← Complex.exp_add]
  congr 1
  push_cast
  ring

/-- The σz ⊗ σz mechanism: unitary conjugation by the flow, as algebra
automorphisms. -/
noncomputable def isingFlow : ℝ → (TwoQubits ≃ₐ[ℂ] TwoQubits) := fun t =>
  unitaryConjAlgEquiv (isingU t) (isingU_mul_conjTranspose_self t)
    (isingU_conjTranspose_mul_self t)

/-- The flow conserves the trace (unitary invariance — the profile's
conservation law, direct form; compare `trace_eq_of_flow` in
`QtslAux.OpenQuantum` for the master-equation form). -/
theorem isingEvolution_trace (ρ₀ : TwoQubits) (t : ℝ) :
    (isingU t * ρ₀ * (isingU t)ᴴ).trace = ρ₀.trace := by
  rw [Matrix.trace_mul_comm, ← Matrix.mul_assoc,
    isingU_conjTranspose_mul_self, one_mul]

/-- The flow preserves positive semidefiniteness: the Heisenberg
determinacy statements are about honest states. -/
theorem isingEvolution_posSemidef {ρ₀ : TwoQubits} (hρ : ρ₀.PosSemidef)
    (t : ℝ) : (isingU t * ρ₀ * (isingU t)ᴴ).PosSemidef := by
  have h := hρ.conjTranspose_mul_mul_same (isingU t)ᴴ
  rwa [Matrix.conjTranspose_conjTranspose] at h

section OdeTie

open scoped Matrix.Norms.Elementwise

/-- Entrywise form of the evolved state: diagonal conjugation is a pure
phase on each matrix entry. -/
private theorem isingEvolution_apply (s : ℝ) (ρ₀ : TwoQubits)
    (a b : Fin 2 × Fin 2) :
    (isingU s * ρ₀ * (isingU s)ᴴ) a b =
      ρ₀ a b * Complex.exp (Complex.I * (s : ℂ) *
        ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))) := by
  unfold isingU
  rw [Matrix.diagonal_conjTranspose, Matrix.mul_diagonal,
    Matrix.diagonal_mul, Pi.star_apply, star_exp_isingArg,
    mul_right_comm, ← Complex.exp_add, mul_comm]
  congr 2
  ring

/-- Collapse of a rank-one expansion at an entry. -/
private theorem sum_smul_single_apply {m : Type*} [Fintype m]
    [DecidableEq m] (c : m → m → ℂ) (a b : m) :
    (∑ i : m, ∑ j : m, c i j • Matrix.single i j (1 : ℂ)) a b = c a b := by
  rw [Matrix.sum_apply]
  rw [Finset.sum_eq_single_of_mem a (Finset.mem_univ a) ?_]
  · rw [Matrix.sum_apply]
    rw [Finset.sum_eq_single_of_mem b (Finset.mem_univ b) ?_]
    · rw [Matrix.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      simp [Matrix.single, Matrix.of_apply]
    · intro j _ hj
      rw [Matrix.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      simp only [Matrix.single, Matrix.of_apply, ite_and]
      rw [if_true, if_neg hj, mul_zero]
  · intro i _ hi
    rw [Matrix.sum_apply]
    refine Finset.sum_eq_zero fun j _ => ?_
    rw [Matrix.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
    simp only [Matrix.single, Matrix.of_apply, ite_and]
    rw [if_neg hi, mul_zero]

/-- **The mechanism profile, consumed on the nose**: the unitary
conjugation flow of the coupling solves the master equation of the
Lindblad-free GKSL profile — the `hflow` hypothesis shape of
`fence_transport` in `QtslAux.OpenQuantum`, discharged for a concrete
interacting mechanism. -/
theorem hasDerivAt_isingEvolution (ρ₀ : TwoQubits) (t : ℝ) :
    HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ => isingU s * ρ₀ * (isingU s)ᴴ)
      (gksl isingH ![] (isingU t * ρ₀ * (isingU t)ᴴ)) t := by
  have hcurve : (fun s : ℝ => isingU s * ρ₀ * (isingU s)ᴴ) =
      fun s : ℝ => ∑ a : Fin 2 × Fin 2, ∑ b : Fin 2 × Fin 2,
        (ρ₀ a b * Complex.exp (Complex.I * (s : ℂ) *
          ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ)))) •
          Matrix.single a b 1 := by
    funext s
    conv_lhs => rw [Matrix.matrix_eq_sum_single (isingU s * ρ₀ * (isingU s)ᴴ)]
    refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun a _ => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun b _ => ?_
    rw [show (ρ₀ a b * Complex.exp (Complex.I * (s : ℂ) *
          ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ)))) •
          Matrix.single a b (1 : ℂ) =
        Matrix.single a b (ρ₀ a b * Complex.exp (Complex.I * (s : ℂ) *
          ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ)))) from by
      rw [Matrix.smul_single, smul_eq_mul, mul_one]]
    exact congrArg (Matrix.single a b) (isingEvolution_apply s ρ₀ a b)
  have hgksl : gksl isingH ![] (isingU t * ρ₀ * (isingU t)ᴴ) =
      ∑ a : Fin 2 × Fin 2, ∑ b : Fin 2 × Fin 2,
        (ρ₀ a b * (Complex.exp (Complex.I * (t : ℂ) *
          ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))) *
          (Complex.I * ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))))) •
          Matrix.single a b 1 := by
    rw [gksl_apply]
    simp only [Finset.univ_eq_empty, Finset.sum_empty, add_zero]
    ext a b
    rw [sum_smul_single_apply, Matrix.smul_apply, Matrix.sub_apply]
    unfold isingH
    rw [Matrix.diagonal_mul, Matrix.mul_diagonal, isingEvolution_apply,
      smul_eq_mul]
    ring
  rw [hcurve, hgksl]
  refine HasDerivAt.fun_sum ?_
  intro a _
  refine HasDerivAt.fun_sum ?_
  intro b _
  have hlin : HasDerivAt
      (fun z : ℂ =>
        Complex.I * z * ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ)))
      (Complex.I * ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))) (t : ℂ) := by
    simpa using ((hasDerivAt_id (t : ℂ)).const_mul Complex.I).mul_const
      ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))
  have hexp : HasDerivAt
      (fun s : ℝ => Complex.exp (Complex.I * (s : ℂ) *
        ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))))
      (Complex.exp (Complex.I * (t : ℂ) *
        ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ))) *
        (Complex.I * ((isingPhase b : ℂ) - (isingPhase a : ℂ)))) t :=
    hlin.cexp.comp_ofReal
  exact (hexp.const_mul (ρ₀ a b)).smul_const (Matrix.single a b (1 : ℂ))

end OdeTie

/-! ## The reversibility fence

The algebra-transport formulation of Cauchy-determinacy is scoped to
the reversible (Lindblad-free) profiles, and the boundary is a theorem
pair: Hamiltonian Heisenberg generators are derivations; one Lindblad
operator breaks the Leibniz law.  Dissipative Heisenberg maps are not
algebra morphisms even infinitesimally (complete positivity is
Lindblad's theorem, Commun. Math. Phys. 48, 119 (1976); morphism-ness
is what it deliberately is not), so no Heisenberg net rides them:
forward state determinacy for dissipative profiles is ODE-uniqueness
territory, out of scope and named. -/

/-- **The Lindblad-free Heisenberg generator is a derivation**: without
dissipators, `gkslDag` obeys the Leibniz law — the infinitesimal form
of "the Hamiltonian flow is by algebra automorphisms". -/
theorem gkslDag_mul_of_isEmpty {ι' : Type*} [Fintype ι'] [IsEmpty ι']
    (H : Matrix n n ℂ) (L : ι' → Matrix n n ℂ) (a b : Matrix n n ℂ) :
    gkslDag H L (a * b) = gkslDag H L a * b + a * gkslDag H L b := by
  simp only [gkslDag_apply, Finset.univ_eq_empty, Finset.sum_empty,
    add_zero, smul_mul_assoc, mul_smul_comm, ← smul_add]
  congr 1
  noncomm_ring

/-- **One Lindblad operator breaks the Leibniz law**: with the
amplitude-damping operator and the excited-state projector,
`gkslDag 0 L (a·a) = −E₁₁` while the Leibniz combination gives
`−2·E₁₁`.  Dissipative Heisenberg generators are not derivations —
the kernel-visible boundary of the reversible fragment. -/
theorem exists_gkslDag_not_leibniz :
    ∃ (L : Fin 1 → Qubit) (a b : Qubit),
      gkslDag 0 L (a * b) ≠ gkslDag 0 L a * b + a * gkslDag 0 L b := by
  refine ⟨![Matrix.single 0 1 1], Matrix.single 1 1 1,
    Matrix.single 1 1 1, ?_⟩
  intro h
  have hc := congrFun (congrFun h 1) 1
  simp only [gkslDag_apply] at hc
  norm_num [Matrix.single, Matrix.of_apply, Matrix.mul_apply,
    Matrix.conjTranspose_apply, Matrix.smul_apply, Matrix.sub_apply,
    Matrix.add_apply, Matrix.zero_apply, Matrix.cons_val_zero,
    Fin.sum_univ_one, Fin.sum_univ_two, Complex.ext_iff] at hc

/-! ## The headline instances: determinacy under the concrete profile -/

/-- The two-qubit Heisenberg net under the σz ⊗ σz mechanism. -/
noncomputable abbrev isingQubitNet :
    ObservableNet (Mink ℝ) (Fin 2 × Fin 2) :=
  heisenbergQubitNet isingFlow

/-- Cauchy-determinacy under the σz ⊗ σz profile, algebra grade. -/
theorem isingQubitNet_slice_determinacy :
    isingQubitNet.alg Eslice = isingQubitNet.alg Dstar :=
  heisenbergQubitNet_alg_Eslice isingFlow

/-- Cauchy-determinacy under the σz ⊗ σz profile, state grade: the
restriction along the Cauchy inclusion is invertible. -/
theorem isingQubitNet_slice_restriction_bijective :
    Function.Bijective (fun x =>
      (StatePresheaf isingQubitNet).map (homOfLE Eslice_le_Dstar).op x) :=
  heisenbergQubitNet_Eslice_restriction_bijective isingFlow

/-- Determinacy transports to every Minkowski translate by pure
covariance transport (`QtslAux.QuantumCovariance`): no slice or matrix
fact is re-proven. -/
theorem isingQubitNet_translate_slice_determinacy (a : ℝ) (v : ℝ) :
    (isingQubitNet.map (minkTranslate a v)).alg
        (Eslice.map (minkTranslate a v)) =
      (isingQubitNet.map (minkTranslate a v)).alg
        (Dstar.map (minkTranslate a v)) :=
  alg_eq_transport (minkTranslate a v) isingQubitNet_slice_determinacy

end QuantumDeterminacy
